231
00:13:18,400 --> 00:13:24,160
 It's made up of a superficial fibrous pericardium and it's made up of a serous pericardium.

232
00:13:24,160 --> 00:13:30,520
 Now, the fibrous pericardium, its main job is to protect, also anchor the heart in place

233
00:13:30,520 --> 00:13:35,840
 to the surrounding structures such as the diaphragm, the sternum, in addition to some

234
00:13:35,840 --> 00:13:37,280
 of the blood vessels that we have there.

235
00:13:37,280 --> 00:13:40,200
 And it also prevents the heart from overfilling with blood.

236
00:13:40,200 --> 00:13:43,640
 As to the serous pericardium, it actually consists of two layers.

237
00:13:43,640 --> 00:13:46,880
 It's made up of a parietal layer and a visceral layer.

238
00:13:46,880 --> 00:13:52,000
 The parietal layer, it lines the internal surface of the fibrous pericardium.

239
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:56,040
 And the visceral layer, which is also known as the epicardium, is found on the external

240
00:13:56,040 --> 00:13:57,540
 surface of the heart.

241
00:13:57,540 --> 00:14:01,760
 Now these two layers, the visceral and the parietal layers, are separated by a fluid-filled

242
00:14:01,760 --> 00:14:04,040
 cavity called a pericardial cavity.

243
00:14:04,040 --> 00:14:08,440
 The pericardial cavity contains serous fluid, which decreases the friction between the two

244
00:14:08,440 --> 00:14:12,640
 layers as the heart contracts.

245
00:14:12,640 --> 00:14:16,440
 So this slide goes over the layers of the pericardium and the heart wall.

246
00:14:16,440 --> 00:14:21,200
 And this is an important slide to understand because the next few slides that we go over

247
00:14:21,200 --> 00:14:24,360
 is going to be in reference to what we look at over here, in addition to what we spoke

248
00:14:24,360 --> 00:14:26,480
 about in the last slide.

249
00:14:26,480 --> 00:14:30,120
 So what we're looking at over here is a part of the heart.

250
00:14:30,120 --> 00:14:33,000
 And this is probably an atria that we're looking at.

251
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,600
 So it's part of this upper chamber of the heart.

252
00:14:35,600 --> 00:14:40,640
 So when you look over here, what you're going to see is right over here, this is the inside

253
00:14:40,640 --> 00:14:41,640
 of the chamber.

254
00:14:41,640 --> 00:14:44,920
 So this is going to be the inside of the atria.

255
00:14:44,920 --> 00:14:52,800
 And when you look at it over here, the roof of this atria, we find this has this endocardium.

256
00:14:52,800 --> 00:14:56,400
 So this is one of the walls of the heart, which is the endocardium.

257
00:14:56,400 --> 00:15:00,400
 Now the second part of the heart is this or the second part of the wall of the heart

258
00:15:00,400 --> 00:15:03,040
 is the myocardium.

259
00:15:03,040 --> 00:15:08,520
 So endocardium, myocardium, and finally we have the epicardium.

260
00:15:08,520 --> 00:15:15,160
 So these are the three layers of the heart, epicardium, myocardium, and the endocardium.

261
00:15:15,160 --> 00:15:19,480
 Okay, so remember after the endocardium what we have, the inside of the chambers of the

262
00:15:19,480 --> 00:15:20,480
 heart.

263
00:15:20,480 --> 00:15:24,680
 Okay, so this is the atria, could have very well been in ventricle.

264
00:15:24,680 --> 00:15:27,760
 But what you're going to find out at ventricles, you're going to find a lot more muscle over

265
00:15:27,760 --> 00:15:30,440
 there, not thin muscle like this.

266
00:15:30,440 --> 00:15:33,200
 So anyways, that's something else we'll be looking at.

267
00:15:33,200 --> 00:15:35,640
 But well here, you can see it over here as well.

268
00:15:35,640 --> 00:15:40,160
 So notice at the ventricle you have a lot more muscle, whereas the atria have very little

269
00:15:40,160 --> 00:15:44,200
 muscle over here, same thing on this side, very little muscle where you have a lot more

270
00:15:44,200 --> 00:15:47,720
 muscle found at the ventricles.

271
00:15:47,720 --> 00:15:52,600
 Now coming back over here, so remember what we're looking at, this is the endocardium.

272
00:15:52,600 --> 00:15:57,760
 Okay, then we have the myocardium, finally we have the epicardium.

273
00:15:57,760 --> 00:16:04,400
 Now when we talk about the fibrous pericardium and the serous pericardium, now let's look

274
00:16:04,400 --> 00:16:05,400
 at these structures.

275
00:16:05,400 --> 00:16:07,280
 We'll talk about that in the last slide.

276
00:16:07,280 --> 00:16:11,840
 So the fibrous pericardium, remember, this is made of this dense connective tissue, and

277
00:16:11,840 --> 00:16:14,280
 this is the outer part of that sac that we're going to find.

278
00:16:14,280 --> 00:16:19,080
 So here it is right over here, this is fibrous pericardium over there and over here.

279
00:16:19,080 --> 00:16:23,960
 Now after you the fibrous pericardium, we find this, the parietal layer of the serous

280
00:16:23,960 --> 00:16:26,320
 pericardium, and this is this part over here.

281
00:16:26,320 --> 00:16:30,600
 So as you see what happens is, this parietal layer, you find it right underneath this fibrous

282
00:16:30,600 --> 00:16:38,320
 pericardium, and this parietal layer of this serous pericardium, it's going to go and it

283
00:16:38,320 --> 00:16:40,080
 turns over, it flips over.

284
00:16:40,080 --> 00:16:46,880
 When it flips over and then it comes into making contact with this myocardium, okay, then this

285
00:16:46,880 --> 00:16:52,640
 is what we refer to as this visceral layer of the serous membrane or epicardium, it's

286
00:16:52,640 --> 00:16:54,040
 the same thing.

287
00:16:54,040 --> 00:17:01,040
 The visceral layer of the serous membrane is also called the epicardium, which is also

288
00:17:01,040 --> 00:17:04,400
 the outermost wall of the heart, okay.

289
00:17:04,400 --> 00:17:07,520
 So and you will see that in the slide that we go over.

290
00:17:07,520 --> 00:17:09,400
 So remember, how does this happen?

291
00:17:09,400 --> 00:17:13,840
 It happens by this parietal layer of the serous pericardium, so this parietal layer, it comes

292
00:17:13,840 --> 00:17:15,360
 over and it flips over.

293
00:17:15,360 --> 00:17:20,280
 When it flips over, it turns into this visceral layer of the serous membrane, okay.

294
00:17:20,280 --> 00:17:24,120
 When this parietal layer flips over, it becomes the epicardium.

295
00:17:24,120 --> 00:17:28,000
 Now what will what happens is, if you notice, you have this space over here.

296
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:35,160
 So when this parietal layer, when it flips over, it forms this cavity right over the space,

297
00:17:35,160 --> 00:17:40,000
 and this is this paracardial cavity and it's over here, within this cavity, we have serous

298
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:41,920
 fluid that's found.

299
00:17:41,920 --> 00:17:47,800
 So as the heart beats, what happens is, these two layers, they slip past one another, so

300
00:17:47,800 --> 00:17:50,400
 you get no friction, okay.

301
00:17:50,400 --> 00:17:53,680
 So sometimes you can have some conditions we're going to be talking about in the next

302
00:17:53,680 --> 00:17:54,920
 few slides.

303
00:17:54,920 --> 00:18:00,680
 When this two layers and they start rubbing up too much, it you end up getting inflammation.

304
00:18:00,680 --> 00:18:05,040
 And then in certain cases, what could happen is that you could end up having fluid that

305
00:18:05,040 --> 00:18:06,360
 starts to accumulate.

306
00:18:06,360 --> 00:18:10,160
 If you start getting fluid accumulating, now what ends up happening is that this heart

307
00:18:10,160 --> 00:18:13,720
 cannot contract anymore because that fluid is starting to push down on this muscle.

308
00:18:13,720 --> 00:18:17,240
 So if you think about it, if you got fluid pushing down on this muscle, it's going to

309
00:18:17,240 --> 00:18:19,200
 be very difficult for it to contract.

310
00:18:19,200 --> 00:18:21,840
 And that's a very, you know, it's a big problem.

311
00:18:21,840 --> 00:18:25,680
 So we'll look at that in the next slide.

312
00:18:25,680 --> 00:18:27,520
 But again, make sure you understand this.

313
00:18:27,520 --> 00:18:28,520
 What do we have here?

314
00:18:28,520 --> 00:18:33,880
 Remember, this is this the epicardium, which is a visceral layer of the serous membrane.

315
00:18:33,880 --> 00:18:37,880
 Then you have the myocardium, then you have the endocardium, then finally we have the

316
00:18:37,880 --> 00:18:39,800
 heart chamber.

317
00:18:39,800 --> 00:18:43,920
 Back over here, remember, do not forget these structures.

318
00:18:43,920 --> 00:18:47,200
 These are very good exam questions that are very likely to be on our dietids, it's just

319
00:18:47,200 --> 00:18:50,720
 inflammation of the pericardium, which roughens the surface of the serous membrane.

320
00:18:50,720 --> 00:18:54,720
 And as the heart beats and rubs against the pericardial sac, it makes a creaking sound,

321
00:18:54,720 --> 00:18:58,240
 which is called a pericardial friction rub, that we can hear with the stethoscope.

322
00:18:58,240 --> 00:19:03,240
 In severe cases, large amounts of inflammatory fluid seep into the pericardial cavity.

323
00:19:03,240 --> 00:19:07,520
 The axis fluid then starts pushing down on the heart, limiting its ability to contract

324
00:19:07,520 --> 00:19:08,960
 and pump blood.

325
00:19:08,960 --> 00:19:12,720
 This is called cardiac tympanate, and the way you treat this condition is with needle

326
00:19:12,720 --> 00:19:13,720
 and syringe.

327
00:19:13,720 --> 00:19:18,080
 A doctor will insert the needle into the pericardial cavity, and aspirate that axis fluid with

328
00:19:18,080 --> 00:19:19,080
 the syringe.

329
00:19:19,080 --> 00:19:21,520
 Now let's take a look at the layers of the heart.

330
00:19:21,520 --> 00:19:27,240
 As we mentioned earlier, there's three layers of the heart wall, the epicardium, the myocardium,

331
00:19:27,240 --> 00:19:28,760
 and the endocardium.

332
00:19:28,760 --> 00:19:31,880
 So let's start off with the outermost layer, which is the epicardium.

333
00:19:31,880 --> 00:19:36,720
 So as we mentioned earlier, this is also the visceral layer of the serous pericardium.

334
00:19:36,720 --> 00:19:40,360
 The epicardium also contains a lot of fat, which you mostly see in older people.

335
00:19:40,360 --> 00:19:42,360
 The middle layer is the myocardium.

336
00:19:42,360 --> 00:19:45,280
 This is a muscle layer of the heart, which forms the bulk of the heart.

337
00:19:45,280 --> 00:19:49,240
 The muscle cells are arranged in circular spiral bundles, and tethered to one another,

338
00:19:49,240 --> 00:19:51,440
 by crisscrossing connective tissue fibers.

339
00:19:51,440 --> 00:19:55,120
 These connective tissue fibers also form a dense network called the fibrous skeleton

340
00:19:55,120 --> 00:19:59,800
 of the heart, which reinforces the myocardium internally, and anchors the cardiac muscle

341
00:19:59,800 --> 00:20:00,800
 fibers.

342
00:20:00,800 --> 00:20:03,880
 It also provides additional support to where the great vessels emerge at the heart and

343
00:20:03,880 --> 00:20:05,240
 are on the valves of the heart.

344
00:20:05,240 --> 00:20:08,400
 And this is quite important, because these structures are under constant pressure from

345
00:20:08,400 --> 00:20:09,800
 the blood moving through them.

346
00:20:09,800 --> 00:20:13,640
 So if they didn't have this, these structures could eventually become stretched.

347
00:20:13,640 --> 00:20:18,120
 And lastly, one of the attributes of connective tissue is that it's not electrically excitable.

348
00:20:18,120 --> 00:20:22,400
 And because of this property, the fiber skeleton limits the direct spread of action potentials

349
00:20:22,400 --> 00:20:25,320
 across the heart to specific pathways.

350
00:20:25,320 --> 00:20:28,800
 And in this slide here, we're looking at the muscle bundles that we find in the heart.

351
00:20:28,800 --> 00:20:31,880
 So when you look over here, these are the circular bundles.

352
00:20:31,880 --> 00:20:32,880
 So this is the atria.

353
00:20:32,880 --> 00:20:34,920
 This is where we would find the atria.

354
00:20:34,920 --> 00:20:39,880
 So what's happening over here in the atria is that you want to push the blood from this

355
00:20:39,880 --> 00:20:41,160
 chamber down to this next chamber.

356
00:20:41,160 --> 00:20:47,000
 So these muscle fibers, they're oriented in a circular fashion, so that when it squeezes,

357
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:51,000
 the content, which is the blood, will get forced down into this direction.

358
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:56,360
 Now when you look over here, these muscle fibers now, they're oriented more in a spiral fashion,

359
00:20:56,360 --> 00:20:57,840
 because this is a ventricle now.

360
00:20:57,840 --> 00:20:59,840
 This is your right ventricle.

361
00:20:59,840 --> 00:21:06,160
 So what's going to happen over here is we want the blood to go into this next blood vessel.

362
00:21:06,160 --> 00:21:09,360
 And this blood vessel that is going to be going to is found somewhere over here.

363
00:21:09,360 --> 00:21:14,120
 So it's not going to be much use if the force that's going to be squeezing that blood out

364
00:21:14,120 --> 00:21:16,480
 is going to be going downwards as it was over here.

365
00:21:16,480 --> 00:21:20,520
 So instead, we have it at an angle, because the blood vessels over here, we want the force

366
00:21:20,520 --> 00:21:21,520
 to go this way.

367
00:21:21,520 --> 00:21:25,760
 And this is why these muscles are arranged in this fashion over here.

368
00:21:25,760 --> 00:21:29,560
 And on this side over here, so for the left ventricle, it's the same as the right ventricle.

369
00:21:29,560 --> 00:21:31,360
 The force needs to be going downwards.

370
00:21:31,360 --> 00:21:35,080
 Now over here, notice that the direction is opposite to this, it's a mirror image, because

371
00:21:35,080 --> 00:21:39,280
 what we want over here is the next blood vessel is going to be somewhere right over here.

372
00:21:39,280 --> 00:21:43,320
 So when this contracts, the blood needs to be pushed up this way, which is why we have

373
00:21:43,320 --> 00:21:46,520
 these fibers arranged in this fashion here, in this spiral fashion.

374
00:21:46,520 --> 00:21:50,880
 Lastly, we come to the third and innermost layer of the heart, which is the endocardium.

375
00:21:50,880 --> 00:21:55,160
 The endocardium consists of a sheet of endothelium that lies on top of a thin layer of connective

376
00:21:55,160 --> 00:21:56,160
 tissue.

377
00:21:56,160 --> 00:22:00,440
 The endocardium is continuous with the endothelial lining of the blood vessels, which enter and

378
00:22:00,440 --> 00:22:01,680
 leave the heart.

379
00:22:01,680 --> 00:22:07,920
 It also lines all the chambers of the heart and covers the fiber skeleton of the valves.

380
00:22:07,920 --> 00:22:11,040
 Coming back to this slide, what we want to look at are the three layers.

381
00:22:11,040 --> 00:22:15,340
 So right over here, this is the epicardium, which is also the visceral layer of the serous

382
00:22:15,340 --> 00:22:16,340
 pericardium.

383
00:22:16,340 --> 00:22:19,800
 Then we have this middle layer, which is the myocardium.

384
00:22:19,800 --> 00:22:22,080
 Then finally, we have the endocardium.

385
00:22:22,080 --> 00:22:24,600
 Now let's look at the internal features of the heart.

386
00:22:24,600 --> 00:22:27,500
 As we mentioned earlier, the heart has four chambers.

387
00:22:27,500 --> 00:22:31,380
 Two chambers on top are called the atria, and the two chambers on the bottom are called

388
00:22:31,380 --> 00:22:32,380
 the ventricles.

389
00:22:32,380 --> 00:22:37,760
 Now between the two chambers, there is a wall that walls called the septum.

390
00:22:37,760 --> 00:22:41,960
 So when we're looking at the wall that's found between the two atria, that's called

391
00:22:41,960 --> 00:22:44,800
 the inter-atrial septum.

392
00:22:44,800 --> 00:22:49,600
 When we're looking at the wall that's found between the ventricles, it's called the inter-ventricular

393
00:22:49,600 --> 00:22:50,600
 septum.

394
00:22:50,600 --> 00:22:54,480
 If you break down the word, it's so much easier to remember and memorize.

395
00:22:54,480 --> 00:22:59,640
 So look at this word, inter means between atrial, it refers to the atria.

396
00:22:59,640 --> 00:23:02,920
 Septum means wall, so inter-atrial septum.

397
00:23:02,920 --> 00:23:08,080
 In this case over here, ventricular refers to the ventricles.

398
00:23:08,080 --> 00:23:11,240
 Inter once again is between, and septum is a wall.

399
00:23:11,240 --> 00:23:18,080
 So the wall that's found between the ventricles, inter-atrial septum, inter-ventricular septum.

400
00:23:18,080 --> 00:23:22,640
 Going back to the ventricles in atria, the right ventricle forms most of the anterior

401
00:23:22,640 --> 00:23:27,960
 surface of the heart, while the left ventricle forms the heart's apex and most of the inferior

402
00:23:27,960 --> 00:23:29,480
 posterior aspects of the heart.

403
00:23:29,480 --> 00:23:34,320
 And when we look at the inter-atrial septum, we find a shallow depression that's called

404
00:23:34,320 --> 00:23:41,760
 the fossa ovelus, which was an opening called the foramen ovale in the fetal heart.

405
00:23:41,760 --> 00:23:45,160
 Moving on to the side over here, this is a cross section that's revealing some of the

406
00:23:45,160 --> 00:23:47,040
 internal structures of the heart.

407
00:23:47,040 --> 00:23:50,160
 So the first thing that we want to look at is this chamber up over here.

408
00:23:50,160 --> 00:23:52,320
 This is our right atrium.

409
00:23:52,320 --> 00:23:55,120
 Going to the left side, we have the left atrium.

410
00:23:55,120 --> 00:23:58,240
 When you go below the left atrium, you have the left ventricle.

411
00:23:58,240 --> 00:24:03,200
 And on the opposite side, when you go below the right atrium, we find the right ventricle.

412
00:24:03,200 --> 00:24:06,760
 Between the right and left ventricle, we have this wall over here.

413
00:24:06,760 --> 00:24:09,080
 This is the inter-ventricular septum.

414
00:24:09,080 --> 00:24:13,120
 Now we can't see the wall between the right and left atria because of this blood vessel

415
00:24:13,120 --> 00:24:14,440
 that we have over here.

416
00:24:14,440 --> 00:24:18,400
 But again, it would be found between these two walls of the atria.

417
00:24:18,400 --> 00:24:20,880
 The next thing we want to look at is this depression here, which is called the fossa

418
00:24:20,880 --> 00:24:21,880
 ovelus.

419
00:24:21,880 --> 00:24:24,000
 And remember, in the fetus, this is called the foramen ovale.

420
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:25,800
 It was in small opening.

421
00:24:25,800 --> 00:24:30,000
 Now this is the tricuspid valve, and we'll be talking about this.

422
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:33,360
 But again, this valve, if you look over here, there's three cusps.

423
00:24:33,360 --> 00:24:36,840
 So it's called the tricuspid, whereas over here, there's only two cusps.

424
00:24:36,840 --> 00:24:40,400
 So this is called the bicuspid valve, also known as the mitral valve.

425
00:24:40,400 --> 00:24:42,440
 Again, we'll come back to looking at this.

426
00:24:42,440 --> 00:24:44,200
 The next we want to look at is right over here.

427
00:24:44,200 --> 00:24:45,200
 These are the pectinated muscles.

428
00:24:45,200 --> 00:24:49,200
 So notice we find this on the anterior wall of the right atrium, and when you go to the

429
00:24:49,200 --> 00:24:52,920
 left atrium, we don't see it present, because in the left atrium, we're going to find them

430
00:24:52,920 --> 00:24:54,160
 in the oracles.

431
00:24:54,160 --> 00:24:57,160
 And let's stop over here for this slide, and we'll come back to it as we learn a little

432
00:24:57,160 --> 00:24:58,160
 bit more structures.

433
00:24:58,160 --> 00:25:00,680
 Moving on to the surface features of the heart.

434
00:25:00,680 --> 00:25:04,600
 There are two visible grooves found on the surface of the heart that indicate the boundaries

435
00:25:04,600 --> 00:25:09,320
 of the four chambers, in addition to carrying the blood vessels supplying the myocardium.

436
00:25:09,320 --> 00:25:14,560
 They are the coronary sulcus and the anterior and posterior interventricular sulky.

437
00:25:14,560 --> 00:25:18,720
 The coronary sulcus, which is also known as the atrial ventricular groove, encircles

438
00:25:18,720 --> 00:25:21,640
 a junction of the atrium ventricles like a crown.

439
00:25:21,640 --> 00:25:27,520
 The anterior interventricular sulcus is found creating the anterior interventricular artery,

440
00:25:27,520 --> 00:25:32,040
 in addition to marking the anterior position of the septum, separating the right and left

441
00:25:32,040 --> 00:25:33,040
 ventricles.

442
00:25:33,040 --> 00:25:37,320
 It continues as a posterior interventricular sulcus, providing a similar landmark on the

443
00:25:37,320 --> 00:25:39,520
 posterior surface of the heart.

444
00:25:39,520 --> 00:25:43,440
 Moving on to this slide, we're looking at the anterior view of the heart, so we're looking

445
00:25:43,440 --> 00:25:45,600
 at the surface structures of the heart.

446
00:25:45,600 --> 00:25:49,760
 The first thing that we notice are this and this, these two structures here, these are

447
00:25:49,760 --> 00:25:52,400
 called the oracles of the atria.

448
00:25:52,400 --> 00:25:53,400
 Oracle means ear.

449
00:25:53,400 --> 00:25:57,560
 Whoever first saw this structure, it looked like an ear to them and they got to name

450
00:25:57,560 --> 00:25:58,560
 it.

451
00:25:58,560 --> 00:26:00,680
 You find it, you get to name whatever you want.

452
00:26:00,680 --> 00:26:07,480
 This is the right oracle of the right atria and this is the left oracle of the left atrium.

453
00:26:07,480 --> 00:26:11,200
 Coming down over here, we want to see this area here.

454
00:26:11,200 --> 00:26:13,320
 This is your right ventricle.

455
00:26:13,320 --> 00:26:18,660
 So is that the right ventricle forms most of the anterior aspect of the heart and when

456
00:26:18,660 --> 00:26:21,520
