905
00:53:08,520 --> 00:53:13,120
 the blood back flows back into the chamber and gets pumped over and over again.

906
00:53:13,120 --> 00:53:18,000
 When valvular stenosis, the heart valve becomes stiff and constricts the opening.

907
00:53:18,000 --> 00:53:22,600
 This usually happens because of scar tissue that forms from a condition such as endocarditis

908
00:53:22,600 --> 00:53:26,960
 or calcium salt deposits, so now the heart has to contract with more force to push the

909
00:53:26,960 --> 00:53:28,600
 blood out of the chamber.

910
00:53:28,600 --> 00:53:33,640
 In both of these conditions, the heart has to work much harder, and over time, this takes

911
00:53:33,640 --> 00:53:36,040
 a toll on the heart, resulting in its weakening.

912
00:53:36,040 --> 00:53:39,560
 So what needs to be done is to replace the defective valve, and usually this valve happens

913
00:53:39,560 --> 00:53:41,160
 even the mitral valve.

914
00:53:41,160 --> 00:53:45,920
 The defective valve gets replaced with either a mechanical valve, a cow or pig valve, or

915
00:53:45,920 --> 00:53:48,040
 a valve from a human cadaver.

916
00:53:48,040 --> 00:53:52,120
 Now it's worth mentioning that the pig and cow valves are chemically treated to prevent

917
00:53:52,120 --> 00:53:53,120
 rejection.

918
00:53:53,120 --> 00:53:56,960
 As to which valve is better, there's not really a clear-cut answer to that.

919
00:53:56,960 --> 00:54:02,000
 The mechanical valve will usually last between 28-25 years, but you have to be on blood thinners

920
00:54:02,000 --> 00:54:03,000
 the whole time.

921
00:54:03,000 --> 00:54:06,000
 Compared to the tissue valve transplants, you really don't need to be on blood thinners

922
00:54:06,000 --> 00:54:07,000
 for that.

923
00:54:07,000 --> 00:54:10,160
 It's up to the doctor to decide perhaps you have another condition that the doctor may

924
00:54:10,160 --> 00:54:12,380
 want you to be on a blood thinner for.

925
00:54:12,380 --> 00:54:17,480
 Also, scientists and researchers are currently trying to use the patient's own cells to

926
00:54:17,480 --> 00:54:20,760
 produce new tissue, new heart valve tissues.

927
00:54:20,760 --> 00:54:24,080
 So how successful that will be, only time will tell.

928
00:54:24,080 --> 00:54:27,960
 Now let's go over the pathway of blood through the heart.

929
00:54:27,960 --> 00:54:30,040
 We start off on the right side of the heart.

930
00:54:30,040 --> 00:54:35,320
 The superior vena cava, inferior vena cava, and the coronary sinus all bring blood back

931
00:54:35,320 --> 00:54:37,880
 to the right atrium of the heart.

932
00:54:37,880 --> 00:54:41,320
 When the pressure in the right atrium is grey enough, it's going to push these tricuspid

933
00:54:41,320 --> 00:54:45,680
 valves open and the blood will fall into the right ventricle.

934
00:54:45,680 --> 00:54:49,600
 When the right ventricle contracts, blood is going to be forced through the pulmonary

935
00:54:49,600 --> 00:54:53,360
 semilunar valves and enter the pulmonary trunk.

936
00:54:53,360 --> 00:54:58,280
 The pulmonary trunk then divides into the right and left pulmonary arteries, which go

937
00:54:58,280 --> 00:55:04,280
 to the lungs, and at the lungs, the carbon dioxide will be unloaded and the oxygen will

938
00:55:04,280 --> 00:55:07,480
 be loaded into the blood.

939
00:55:07,480 --> 00:55:13,200
 The freshly oxygenated blood will then travel by way of the four pulmonary veins.

940
00:55:13,200 --> 00:55:19,400
 The right and left pulmonary veins returns the blood back to the left atrium of the heart.

941
00:55:19,400 --> 00:55:23,480
 From the left atrium, blood is going to pass through the mitral valves and into the left

942
00:55:23,480 --> 00:55:25,640
 ventricle.

943
00:55:25,640 --> 00:55:30,000
 When the left ventricles contract, the blood will pass through the eoridic semilunar valves

944
00:55:30,000 --> 00:55:36,960
 and enter the aorta, and from the aorta, blood will enter systemic circulation.

945
00:55:36,960 --> 00:55:40,560
 This slide here along with the next illustrates a flow of blood through the heart.

946
00:55:40,560 --> 00:55:44,320
 Looking at the legend over here, the blood represents the oxygenated blood, while the

947
00:55:44,320 --> 00:55:47,360
 right represents oxygenated blood.

948
00:55:47,360 --> 00:55:51,040
 So again, the flow of blood starts on the right side of the heart.

949
00:55:51,040 --> 00:55:55,400
 So we have the superior vena cava, the inferior vena cava, and the coronary signs, which are

950
00:55:55,400 --> 00:56:00,000
 going to return blood back to the right chamber of the heart, the right atrium.

951
00:56:00,000 --> 00:56:04,400
 Now when the right atrium fills up with blood, the next thing that is going to happen is

952
00:56:04,400 --> 00:56:09,720
 that it's going to force this tricuspid valve to open up and enter the right ventricle.

953
00:56:09,720 --> 00:56:14,080
 Remember, this happens when the pressure is greater in the right atrium than it is in

954
00:56:14,080 --> 00:56:16,360
 the right ventricle.

955
00:56:16,360 --> 00:56:20,560
 Now once the right ventricle is full of blood, the next thing that's going to happen is

956
00:56:20,560 --> 00:56:22,840
 this right ventricle will contract.

957
00:56:22,840 --> 00:56:26,880
 When the right ventricle contracts and the pressure is greater in the right ventricle

958
00:56:26,880 --> 00:56:32,360
 than it is in the pulmonary trunk, these pulmonary semilunar valves will open up and

959
00:56:32,360 --> 00:56:36,220
 blood will then fill up this pulmonary trunk.

960
00:56:36,220 --> 00:56:42,040
 From the pulmonary trunk, the blood will then go to the right pulmonary artery and the left

961
00:56:42,040 --> 00:56:45,560
 pulmonary artery to the lungs.

962
00:56:45,560 --> 00:56:51,320
 In the lungs, the carbon dioxide will unload and oxygen will be loaded into the blood and

963
00:56:51,320 --> 00:56:56,520
 then it's going to end up returning back to the heart by way of the right and left pulmonary

964
00:56:56,520 --> 00:56:57,520
 veins.

965
00:56:57,520 --> 00:57:02,120
 So on this side here you can see the right and left pulmonary veins returning blood back

966
00:57:02,120 --> 00:57:04,640
 to the left atrium.

967
00:57:04,640 --> 00:57:08,600
 When the left atrium fills up with blood and the pressure is greater in the left atrium

968
00:57:08,600 --> 00:57:14,400
 than it is in the left ventricle, the bicuspid or mitral valves will force open and the blood

969
00:57:14,400 --> 00:57:17,520
 will fill the left ventricle.

970
00:57:17,520 --> 00:57:22,800
 When the left ventricle is full of blood and this chamber is contracting and the pressure

971
00:57:22,800 --> 00:57:27,780
 is greater in this chamber at that point than it is in the aorta, blood will then force

972
00:57:27,780 --> 00:57:31,460
 through the aortic semilunar valve and enter the aorta.

973
00:57:31,460 --> 00:57:37,040
 And from the aorta, blood will enter a systemic circulation where it's going to deliver the

974
00:57:37,040 --> 00:57:41,680
 oxygenated blood to the tissues of the body and it's going to end up picking up the carbon

975
00:57:41,680 --> 00:57:42,680
 dioxide.

976
00:57:42,680 --> 00:57:47,320
 And from there, the blood is going to make its return trip back to the heart.

977
00:57:47,320 --> 00:57:50,640
 And this slide over here is an overview of the previous two slides.

978
00:57:50,640 --> 00:57:55,560
 So once again, the superior vena cava, the inferior vena cava and the coronary sinus

979
00:57:55,560 --> 00:57:59,480
 bring blood back to this right atrium of the heart.

980
00:57:59,480 --> 00:58:03,200
 When the right atrium of the heart fills up with blood and the pressure is greater in

981
00:58:03,200 --> 00:58:08,640
 the right atrium than it is in the right ventricle, blood will pass through the tricuspid valve

982
00:58:08,640 --> 00:58:12,440
 and fill the right ventricle with blood.

983
00:58:12,440 --> 00:58:18,120
 Now when the right ventricle is full of blood and when the right ventricle is contracting,

984
00:58:18,120 --> 00:58:21,780
 the pressure is going to be greater in the right ventricle than it is going to be in

985
00:58:21,780 --> 00:58:24,080
 the pulmonary trunk.

986
00:58:24,080 --> 00:58:30,280
 When that happens, the pulmonary semilunar valves open up allowing the blood to enter

987
00:58:30,280 --> 00:58:35,840
 the pulmonary trunk and then move into the right and left pulmonary arteries.

988
00:58:35,840 --> 00:58:39,440
 The right and left pulmonary arteries will take the blood to the lungs where it's going

989
00:58:39,440 --> 00:58:43,720
 to unload the carbon dioxide and load on the oxygen.

990
00:58:43,720 --> 00:58:48,640
 And there, blood is going to return back to the left atrium of the heart by way of the

991
00:58:48,640 --> 00:58:51,700
 right and left pulmonary veins.

992
00:58:51,700 --> 00:58:57,760
 When blood is full in the left atrium and the pressure is greater in the left atrium

993
00:58:57,760 --> 00:59:02,080
 than it is in the left ventricle, what's going to happen next is that the blood is going

994
00:59:02,080 --> 00:59:04,040
 to pass through these mitral valves.

995
00:59:04,040 --> 00:59:08,080
 It's going to force this way open, cause these mitral valves to open up and fill up

996
00:59:08,080 --> 00:59:09,960
 into the left ventricle.

997
00:59:09,960 --> 00:59:13,740
 Now when the left ventricle contracts and the pressure in the left ventricle is greater

998
00:59:13,740 --> 00:59:18,280
 than the aorta, the blood is going to pass through the aorta semilunar valves and enter

999
00:59:18,280 --> 00:59:19,820
 the aorta.

1000
00:59:19,820 --> 00:59:23,320
 From the aorta, blood is going to enter a systemic circulation where blood is going to

1001
00:59:23,320 --> 00:59:28,240
 supply the tissues of the body with oxygen and remove the carbon dioxide.

1002
00:59:28,240 --> 00:59:33,600
 And from that point on, the blood will return its journey back to the heart.

1003
00:59:33,600 --> 00:59:36,480
 Once again, I want to point out to you to keep in mind that the right side of the heart

1004
00:59:36,480 --> 00:59:41,760
 is a pulmonary circuit pump while the left side of the heart is a systemic circuit pump.

1005
00:59:41,760 --> 00:59:45,520
 And keep in mind what I told you about arteries in veins earlier on.

1006
00:59:45,520 --> 00:59:50,240
 Always think of arteries as pumping blood away from the heart and veins bringing blood

1007
00:59:50,240 --> 00:59:51,760
 back to the heart.

1008
00:59:51,760 --> 00:59:55,960
 When you look at the heart, never think about veins carrying deoxygenated blood and arteries

1009
00:59:55,960 --> 00:59:57,920
 carrying oxygenated blood.

1010
00:59:57,920 --> 01:00:03,520
 Remember, the right and left pulmonary arteries carry deoxygenated blood while the right and

1011
01:00:03,520 --> 01:00:07,800
 left pulmonary veins carry oxygenated blood.

1012
01:00:07,800 --> 01:00:12,200
 This is the only exception in the body where you'll find veins carrying oxygenated blood

1013
01:00:12,200 --> 01:00:15,400
 while the arteries carry deoxygenated blood.

1014
01:00:15,400 --> 01:00:19,280
 With that said, while at any given moment we have equal volumes of blood that are being

1015
01:00:19,280 --> 01:00:23,880
 pumped to the pulmonary and systemic circuits, the workloads of the ventricles are uneven.

1016
01:00:23,880 --> 01:00:28,280
 The right ventricle which serves the pulmonary circuit is a short low pressure circuit.

1017
01:00:28,280 --> 01:00:31,920
 Remember, when the right ventricle contracts, blood doesn't need to go too far.

1018
01:00:31,920 --> 01:00:36,100
 The blood is being pumped to the lungs which is in close proximity to the heart.

1019
01:00:36,100 --> 01:00:40,080
 When you go to the left side of the heart, the left ventricle serves the systemic circuit

1020
01:00:40,080 --> 01:00:42,960
 which is a long pathway through the entire body.

1021
01:00:42,960 --> 01:00:47,160
 The blood is being pumped against gravity to the face and brain, is being pumped to the

1022
01:00:47,160 --> 01:00:49,440
 extremities reaching our fingers and toes.

1023
01:00:49,440 --> 01:00:54,960
 So this circuit is in covering much more resistance to blood flow, about 5 times as much friction.

1024
01:00:54,960 --> 01:00:58,840
 And these functional differences are evident in the anatomy of the ventricles.

1025
01:00:58,840 --> 01:01:03,940
 As we saw in the previous slides and had mentioned, the walls of the left ventricle are 3 times

1026
01:01:03,940 --> 01:01:06,120
 thicker than that of the right ventricle.

1027
01:01:06,120 --> 01:01:10,280
 So the left ventricle is able to generate much more pressure than the right ventricle

1028
01:01:10,280 --> 01:01:13,360
 which makes it a much more powerful pump.

1029
01:01:13,360 --> 01:01:17,320
 So on this slide over here, notice how much thicker the walls of the left ventricle are

1030
01:01:17,320 --> 01:01:19,600
 compared to that of the right ventricle.

1031
01:01:19,600 --> 01:01:22,760
 Additionally, look at the shape of the right ventricle.

1032
01:01:22,760 --> 01:01:27,320
 This has more of a crescent shape while the left ventricle has more of a circular shape

1033
01:01:27,320 --> 01:01:31,680
 and it's both of these differences that allow the left ventricle to be a much more powerful

1034
01:01:31,680 --> 01:01:34,480
 pump than that of the right ventricle.

1035
01:01:34,480 --> 01:01:38,320
 While the heart is constantly filled with blood in the chambers, it provides very little

1036
01:01:38,320 --> 01:01:40,400
 nourishment to the heart tissue itself.

1037
01:01:40,400 --> 01:01:43,000
 So how does the heart receive its oxygen and nutrients?

1038
01:01:43,000 --> 01:01:46,560
 The answer to that question is through the coronary circulation.

1039
01:01:46,560 --> 01:01:49,920
 Coronary circulation is a functional blood supply of the heart, in addition to being

1040
01:01:49,920 --> 01:01:51,920
 the shortest circulation of the body.

1041
01:01:51,920 --> 01:01:55,960
 As after the blood circulates around the heart, it gets delivered back into the right

1042
01:01:55,960 --> 01:01:58,080
 atrium when the heart is relaxed.

1043
01:01:58,080 --> 01:02:02,840
 And without surprise, the left ventricle receives most of the coronary blood supply.

1044
01:02:02,840 --> 01:02:06,920
 The right and left coronary arteries both arise from the base of the aorta and then circle

1045
01:02:06,920 --> 01:02:09,280
 the heart in the coronary sulcus.

1046
01:02:09,280 --> 01:02:13,880
 These are the blood vessels that provide the arterial supply of the coronary circulation.

1047
01:02:13,880 --> 01:02:17,520
 The branching of the coronary arteries also varies among individuals.

1048
01:02:17,520 --> 01:02:23,640
 For example, in 15% of the population, the left coronary artery gives rise to both intraventricular

1049
01:02:23,640 --> 01:02:28,440
 arteries, and in about 4% of the people, a single coronary artery supplies the entire

1050
01:02:28,440 --> 01:02:29,440
 heart.

1051
01:02:29,440 --> 01:02:34,200
 We also find many anisemoses or junctions among the coronary arterial branches, and while these

1052
01:02:34,200 --> 01:02:37,760
 function to provide additional rods for blood delivery to the heart muscle, they aren't

1053
01:02:37,760 --> 01:02:41,680
 enough to supply enough oxygen and nutrients when the coronary artery is blocked.

1054
01:02:41,680 --> 01:02:46,000
 And unfortunately, when you have a complete block of a coronary artery, it leads to tissue

1055
01:02:46,000 --> 01:02:47,680
 death and a heart attack.

1056
01:02:47,680 --> 01:02:51,320
 It's also worth mentioning that while the heart only accounts for about 1-200 of the total

1057
01:02:51,320 --> 01:02:55,740
 body weight, it requires 1-20 of the body's blood supply.

1058
01:02:55,740 --> 01:02:59,400
 The left coronary artery runs towards the left side of the heart and then divides into

1059
01:02:59,400 --> 01:03:04,560
 two major branches, which are the anterior interventricular artery and the circumflex

1060
01:03:04,560 --> 01:03:05,560
 artery.

1061
01:03:05,560 --> 01:03:10,840
 Additionally, the left coronary artery supplies the intraventricular septum, the anterior ventricular

1062
01:03:10,840 --> 01:03:15,560
 walls, the left atrium, and the posterior wall of the left ventricle.

1063
01:03:15,560 --> 01:03:19,760
 The anterior interventricular artery, which is also known as the left anterior de-centering

1064
01:03:19,760 --> 01:03:25,480
 artery, follows the anterior interventricular septum, and supplies blood to the interventricular

1065
01:03:25,480 --> 01:03:28,880
 septum and anterior walls of both ventricles.

1066
01:03:28,880 --> 01:03:33,600
 The circumflex artery supplies the left atrium and the posterior walls of the left ventricle.

1067
01:03:33,600 --> 01:03:37,600
 The right coronary artery crosses to the right side of the heart, where it also gives rise

1068
01:03:37,600 --> 01:03:43,760
 to two major branches, which are the right marginal artery and the posterior interventricular

1069
01:03:43,760 --> 01:03:44,760
 artery.

1070
01:03:44,760 --> 01:03:49,920
 Additionally, the right coronary artery supplies the right atrium and most of the right ventricle.

1071
01:03:49,920 --> 01:03:54,320
 The right marginal artery serves the myocardium of the lateral right side of the heart, while

1072
01:03:54,320 --> 01:03:59,640
 the posterior interventricular artery runs to the heart's apex and supplies the posterior

1073
01:03:59,640 --> 01:04:00,640
 ventricular walls.

1074
01:04:00,640 --> 01:04:04,720
 And close to the heart's apex, the artery merges with the anterior interventricular

1075
01:04:04,720 --> 01:04:05,720
 artery.

1076
01:04:05,720 --> 01:04:10,080
 So in this slide over here, we have the picture of the heart, and we can see the arteries over

1077
01:04:10,080 --> 01:04:11,080
 here.

1078
01:04:11,080 --> 01:04:15,120
 Since this is the right side of the heart, here we have the right coronary artery, and

1079
01:04:15,120 --> 01:04:17,840
 over here we have the left coronary artery.

1080
01:04:17,840 --> 01:04:23,000
 So following the right coronary artery, you'll notice we have the right marginal artery that

1081
01:04:23,000 --> 01:04:28,040
 comes off over here, and also we have the posterior interventricular artery that comes

1082
01:04:28,040 --> 01:04:29,280
 off of it.

1083
01:04:29,280 --> 01:04:33,680
 And then on the left side, for the left coronary artery, we have the circumflex arteries that

1084
01:04:33,680 --> 01:04:39,560
 come off of it, and then as we continue downwards, we have the anterior interventricular artery,

1085
01:04:39,560 --> 01:04:43,400
 and the anterior interventricular artery, and the posterior interventricular artery.

1086
01:04:43,400 --> 01:04:45,840
 They end up connecting with one another.

1087
01:04:45,840 --> 01:04:50,740
 So over here, this is an anisomosis, you can see here, the other structures, let's see.

1088
01:04:50,740 --> 01:04:55,800
 So here, this is the left ventricle, and this area over here is the right ventricle.

1089
01:04:55,800 --> 01:04:59,560
 Here's the right atrium, this is the oracle of the right atrium, and here's the oracle

1090
01:04:59,560 --> 01:05:01,600
 of the left atrium.

1091
01:05:01,600 --> 01:05:03,440
 This is the pulmonary trunk we have here.

1092
01:05:03,440 --> 01:05:08,400
 This is the aorta, and this is the superior vena cava that you see over here.

1093
01:05:08,400 --> 01:05:12,800
 So after the oxygen-rich arterial blood passes through the capillaries of the myocardium,

1094
01:05:12,800 --> 01:05:15,680
 the venous blood gets picked up by the cardiac veins.

1095
01:05:15,680 --> 01:05:20,640
 The coronary veins follow a path very similar to those of the coronary arteries.

1096
01:05:20,640 --> 01:05:26,320
 The coronary veins come together to form this large blood vessel called the coronary sinus.

1097
01:05:26,320 --> 01:05:30,000
 And if you remember from a handful of slides ago, the coronary sinus is the blood vessel

1098
01:05:30,000 --> 01:05:32,400
 which empties into the right atrium.

1099
01:05:32,400 --> 01:05:36,600
 The three large veins that connect to form the coronary sinus are the great cardiac vein,

1100
01:05:36,600 --> 01:05:41,400
 which is found in the anterior interventricular sulcus, the middle cardiac vein that's found

1101
01:05:41,400 --> 01:05:46,480
 in the posterior interventricular sulcus, and the small cardiac vein, which is found running

1102
01:05:46,480 --> 01:05:49,480
 along the right inferior margin of the heart.

1103
01:05:49,480 --> 01:05:54,560
 We also find several anterior cardiac veins which empty directly into the anterior aspect

1104
01:05:54,560 --> 01:05:55,560
 of the right atrium.

1105
01:05:55,560 --> 01:06:00,120
 In this side here, you can see the great cardiac vein, the middle cardiac vein, and the small

1106
01:06:00,120 --> 01:06:05,720
 cardiac vein merging to form this coronary sinus, which will...

1107
01:06:05,720 --> 01:06:09,800
 In this side here, you can see the great cardiac vein, the middle cardiac vein, and the small

1108
01:06:09,800 --> 01:06:13,240
 cardiac vein, which ends up...

1109
01:06:13,240 --> 01:06:17,600
 In this side here, we can see the great cardiac vein which is coming off the anterior interventricular

1110
01:06:17,600 --> 01:06:23,000
 sulcus, the middle cardiac vein which is coming off the posterior interventricular sulcus,

1111
01:06:23,000 --> 01:06:27,200
 and the small cardiac vein which is coming off the inferior margin to form this coronary

1112
01:06:27,200 --> 01:06:31,800
 sinus, and the coronary sinus will empty into the right atrium.

1113
01:06:31,800 --> 01:06:36,320
 Also we see these anterior cardiac veins which directly empty into the right atrium.

1114
01:06:36,320 --> 01:06:39,360
 Here we have a very good view of the posterior aspect of the heart.

1115
01:06:39,360 --> 01:06:43,320
 So here's the inferior vena cava here, we have the superior vena cava over here, which

1116
01:06:43,320 --> 01:06:46,080
 are both connected to this right atrium.

1117
01:06:46,080 --> 01:06:50,440
 You can see the aorta over here, here's our left pulmonary artery, and the right pulmonary

1118
01:06:50,440 --> 01:06:55,880
 artery here, here we have the left pulmonary veins, and the right pulmonary veins here.

1119
01:06:55,880 --> 01:07:01,880
 And here we can see the oracle of the left atrium, and here's the right atrium over here,

1120
01:07:01,880 --> 01:07:04,320
 and here is the apex of the heart.

1121
01:07:04,320 --> 01:07:10,920
 So coming off the apex, going up we can see right over here, this is going to be our middle

1122
01:07:10,920 --> 01:07:16,240
 cardiac vein, and over here we can see our great cardiac vein.

1123
01:07:16,240 --> 01:07:20,640
 Here's the coronary sinus over here, this is the posterior vein of the left ventricle

